\title{The Berggren Causal Set:\\ Pythagorean Triples as a Discrete Structure on the Null Cone}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Berggren (Barning--Hall) tree generates every primitive Pythagorean triple exactly once
from the seed $(3,4,5)$ by three integer matrices. Since a Pythagorean triple $(a,b,c)$
satisfies $a^2+b^2-c^2=0$, every node of the tree is an integral null vector of the Lorentz
form $Q(x,y,t)=x^2+y^2-t^2$ of signature $(2,1)$, and the three generators lie in
$O(2,1;\ZZ)$. This suggests the hypothesis that the Berggren tree, ordered by ancestry, is a
discrete model of $2{+}1$-dimensional Minkowski spacetime in the sense of causal set theory.
We settle the hypothesis in both directions. Positively, the ancestry order satisfies the
Bombelli--Lee--Meyer--Sorkin axioms (partial order, no closed causal curves, local
finiteness), driven by a unique-parent property derived from a single explicit inverse map
whose sign pattern distinguishes the three branches; the geometry is genuinely Lorentzian,
the move monoid is free of rank three inside $O(2,1;\ZZ)$, levels have exactly $3^k$ events
and are antichains, intervals are chains of exactly $k+1$ events, link lengths are exactly
$4(c-b)^2$, $4(a-b)^2$, $4(c-a)^2$, and the Pell spine is a uniformly spaced geodesic whose
celestial directions converge to the irrational boundary point $\sqrt2/2$. Negatively, any
two distinct events are \emph{spacelike} separated, so no tree edge is an ambient causal
relation; and interval cardinalities are exactly $k+1$, so no Myrheim--Meyer dimension
$\ge 2$ exists and the effective dimension is $1$, while the $3^k$ level growth is
superpolynomial and hence not a volume law. The obstruction is general: pairwise
non-proportional points of a null cone form a total antichain of the ambient causal order.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Two traditions meeting on one equation}

The equation $a^2+b^2=c^2$ belongs to two mathematical cultures at once. In number theory it
defines the Pythagorean triples. In physics, rewritten as
\[
  Q(a,b,c) := a^2+b^2-c^2 = 0,
\]
it defines the \emph{null cone} of $2{+}1$-dimensional Minkowski space $\RR^{2,1}$: the set of
events joined to the origin by a light ray. Every Pythagorean triple is an integer point of
that cone.

Causal set theory (Bombelli--Lee--Meyer--Sorkin) proposes that spacetime is fundamentally a
discrete, locally finite partial order from which the Lorentzian metric is reconstructed by
counting: \emph{order plus number equals geometry}. A candidate discrete spacetime should be
a locally finite poset which embeds faithfully in a Lorentzian manifold, matching
cardinalities with volumes.

The Berggren tree of primitive Pythagorean triples is an attractive candidate: an explicit
infinite ternary tree, lying exactly on the null cone, whose generators preserve $Q$ exactly,
with a famous exponential growth rate governed by the silver ratio $1+\sqrt2$.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Hypothesis (H).} The Berggren tree with the ancestry order is a causal set which is a
discrete model of $2{+}1$-dimensional Minkowski space: its tree edges are causal relations of
$\RR^{2,1}$, and its growth exponent reproduces the spacetime dimension $3$.
\end{quote}

\subsection{Results}

Hypothesis (H) splits into one order-theoretic and two geometric claims; we prove the first
and disprove both others.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Theorem A.} The ancestry order is a locally finite partial order with no closed
  causal curves (Theorems~\ref{thm:po}, \ref{thm:nocc}, Corollary~\ref{cor:locfin}).
\item \textbf{Theorem B.} Events are null vectors; the move monoid acts by integral Lorentz
  matrices; the tree is a single orbit; the monoid is free of rank $3$
  (Section~\ref{sec:lorentz}).
\item \textbf{Theorem C.} Level $k$ has $3^k$ events and is an antichain; every interval of
  proper time $k$ is a chain with $k+1$ events; the link lengths are exact
  (Sections~\ref{sec:order}, \ref{sec:geom}).
\item \textbf{Theorem D.} Any two distinct events are spacelike separated
  (Theorem~\ref{thm:spacelike}).
\item \textbf{Theorem E.} No Myrheim--Meyer dimension $\ge 2$ exists; the effective dimension
  is $1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:dim}); level growth is superpolynomial
  (Theorem~\ref{thm:superpoly}).
\item \textbf{Theorem F.} The Pell spine is a uniformly spaced geodesic whose celestial
  directions converge to the irrational point $\sqrt2/2$ (Section~\ref{sec:boundary}).
\end{itemize}

\section{The Berggren tree}

\begin{definition}[Berggren moves]
For $v=(a,b,c)^{\mathsf T}\in\ZZ^3$ define
\[
Av=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2&2\\2&-1&2\\2&-2&3\end{pmatrix}v,\quad
Bv=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&2\\2&1&2\\2&2&3\end{pmatrix}v,\quad
Cv=\begin{pmatrix}-1&2&2\\-2&1&2\\-2&2&3\end{pmatrix}v .
\]
We call $A$, $B$, $C$ the \emph{outer}, \emph{middle} and \emph{co-outer} moves and set
$S=\{A,B,C\}$. For a word $w=s_1\cdots s_n\in S^{*}$ we write $w\cdot v$ for the result of
applying $s_1$ first.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Events]
An \emph{event} is $t=(a,b,c)\in\ZZ^3$ with $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $a,b,c>0$; it is
\emph{primitive} if $\gcd(a,b)=1$. The \emph{root} is $r=(3,4,5)$.
\end{definition}

The classical Berggren--Barning--Hall theorem says the orbit of $r$ under the free monoid on
$S$ is exactly the set of primitive events, each reached by exactly one word; we reprove the
uniqueness half below, as it drives everything else.

\begin{lemma}[Legs are short]\label{lem:legs}
If $(a,b,c)$ is an event then $a<c$ and $b<c$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$a^2=c^2-b^2<c^2$ with all entries positive; symmetrically for $b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Closure]\label{lem:closure}
If $t$ is an event and $s\in S$ then $st$ is an event, primitive if $t$ is.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Preservation of $Q$ is Lemma~\ref{lem:lorentzgen}. Positivity follows from
Lemma~\ref{lem:legs}: e.g.\ for $A$, $a-2b+2c=a+2(c-b)>0$, $2a-b+2c=b+2(a+c-b)>0$ and
$2a-2b+3c\ge 3c-2b>0$. Primitivity is preserved since a common prime factor of the new legs
pulls back, via the explicit inverse of Definition~\ref{def:parent}, to a common factor of the
old ones.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Time increases]\label{lem:time}
For every event $t=(a,b,c)$ and $s\in S$ one has $(st)_3\ge c+1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(At)_3-c=2(a-b)+2c$, $(Bt)_3-c=2(a+b)+2c$, $(Ct)_3-c=2(b-a)+2c$; each is positive by
Lemma~\ref{lem:legs}, and integrality gives $\ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:mono}
If $|w|=n$ then $(w\cdot t)_3\ge t_3+n$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The unique parent property and the tree theorem}

\begin{definition}[Parent map]\label{def:parent}
$P(a,b,c):=(a+2b-2c,\;2a+b-2c,\;-2a-2b+3c)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Sign pattern]\label{lem:sign}
For all $(a,b,c)\in\ZZ^3$,
\[
P(A(a,b,c))=(a,-b,c),\qquad P(B(a,b,c))=(a,b,c),\qquad P(C(a,b,c))=(-a,b,c).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Three polynomial identities. For $A$: the components of $P(A(a,b,c))$ are
$(a-2b+2c)+2(2a-b+2c)-2(2a-2b+3c)=a$, $2(a-2b+2c)+(2a-b+2c)-2(2a-2b+3c)=-b$, and
$-2(a-2b+2c)-2(2a-b+2c)+3(2a-2b+3c)=c$.
\end{proof}

Thus $P$ inverts $B$ exactly, and on the other two branches returns the parent with a single
sign flipped; since events have positive legs, the location of the minus sign identifies the
branch.

\begin{theorem}[Unique parent]\label{thm:uniqueparent}
Let $t,t'$ be events and $s,s'\in S$. If $st=s't'$ then $s=s'$ and $t=t'$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply $P$ to both sides and use Lemma~\ref{lem:sign}. Writing $t=(a,b,c)$, $t'=(a',b',c')$
with $a,b,a',b'>0$: the diagonal cases give $t=t'$ immediately; the case $(A,B)$ yields
$b'=-b<0$, the case $(A,C)$ yields $-b=b'<0$, the case $(B,C)$ yields $a=-a'<0$, all
contradictions, and the transposed cases are symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Causal relation]
For events $t,u$ write $t\preceq u$ iff $u=w\cdot t$ for some $w\in S^{*}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Ancestor factorisation]\label{lem:factor}
If $t,t'$ are events, $|w|\le|w'|$ and $w\cdot t=w'\cdot t'$, then $w'=p\,w$ for some word
$p$ with $p\cdot t'=t$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $|w|$. If $|w|=0$ take $p=w'$. Otherwise write $w=r\,s$, $w'=r'\,s'$ with final
letters $s,s'$; then $s(r\cdot t)=s'(r'\cdot t')$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqueparent} (both
arguments are events by Lemma~\ref{lem:closure}) gives $s=s'$ and $r\cdot t=r'\cdot t'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tree property]\label{thm:tree}
For an event $t$ and words $w,w'$: if $w\cdot t=w'\cdot t$ then $w=w'$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Assume $|w|\le|w'|$. Lemma~\ref{lem:factor} gives $p$ with $w'=pw$ and $p\cdot t=t$; by
Corollary~\ref{cor:mono}, $t_3\ge t_3+|p|$, so $|p|=0$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Lorentz structure}\label{sec:lorentz}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:lorentzgen}
With $\QL=\diag(1,1,-1)$, so that $Q(v)=v^{\mathsf T}\QL v$, one has
$M^{\mathsf T}\QL M=\QL$ for $M\in\{A,B,C\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Lorentz orbit]\label{thm:orbit}
For every word $w$, the associated product $M_w$ of generator matrices satisfies
$M_w^{\mathsf T}\QL M_w=\QL$, i.e.\ $M_w\in O(2,1;\ZZ)$, and $M_w v_t=v_{w\cdot t}$ for every
event $t$. Moreover $\det M_w=(-1)^{\#_B(w)}$, where $\#_B(w)$ counts middle moves.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $|w|$, using
$(MN)^{\mathsf T}\QL(MN)=N^{\mathsf T}(M^{\mathsf T}\QL M)N$ and multiplicativity of the
determinant, with $\det A=\det C=1$ and $\det B=-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Every event is a future-directed null vector, and the move monoid acts on the null cone by
integral Lorentz transformations.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Freeness]\label{thm:free}
The map $w\mapsto M_w$ is injective; hence $A,B,C$ generate a free monoid of rank three
inside $O(2,1;\ZZ)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$M_w=M_{w'}$ implies $w\cdot r=w'\cdot r$, hence $w=w'$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:tree}.
\end{proof}

\section{Order theory: the causal set axioms}\label{sec:order}

\begin{theorem}[Partial order]\label{thm:po}
On events, $\preceq$ is reflexive, transitive and antisymmetric.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Reflexivity: empty word. Transitivity: concatenation. Antisymmetry: $u=w_1\cdot t$ and
$t=w_2\cdot u$ give $(w_1w_2)\cdot t=t$, so $|w_1w_2|=0$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:mono}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No closed causal curves]\label{thm:nocc}
For every event $t$ and nonempty word $w$, $w\cdot t\ne t$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(w\cdot t)_3\ge t_3+|w|>t_3$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:mono}.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
The causal interval is $[t,u]:=\{x: t\preceq x\preceq u\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Interval description]\label{thm:interval}
Let $u=v\cdot t$ with $|v|=k$. Then $[t,u]=\{(v_{\le j})\cdot t : 0\le j\le k\}$, where
$v_{\le j}$ is the length-$j$ prefix, and the $k+1$ listed events are pairwise distinct.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $x\in[t,u]$, write $x=w\cdot t$ and $u=w'\cdot x$; then $(ww')\cdot t=v\cdot t$, so
$ww'=v$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:tree} and $w$ is a prefix of $v$. Conversely every prefix
qualifies. Distinctness: for $i<j$ the third coordinate strictly increases along the segment
of $v$ from position $i$ to $j$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:mono}).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Local finiteness]\label{cor:locfin}
$[t,u]$ is finite for all events $t,u$ (empty if $t\not\preceq u$).
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Intervals are chains]\label{thm:chain}
Any two elements of $[t,u]$ are causally related.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both are prefixes of the same word; the intermediate segment relates them.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Proper time]
For $t\preceq u$ let $\tau(t,u)$ be the length of the unique connecting word
(Theorem~\ref{thm:tree}).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Proper time]\label{thm:propertime}
$\tau$ is additive along causal chains, satisfies $t_3+\tau(t,u)\le u_3$, computes interval
volumes as $|[t,u]|=\tau(t,u)+1$, and $\tau(t,u)=1$ holds precisely when $u=st$ for a single
move $s$: the links of the causal set are exactly the Berggren moves.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Levels]\label{thm:levels}
The set $L_k(t)$ of depth-$k$ descendants of an event $t$ has exactly $3^k$ elements and is an
antichain.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$w\mapsto w\cdot t$ from $S^k$ is surjective by definition and injective by
Theorem~\ref{thm:tree}. If $w\cdot t\preceq w'\cdot t$ with $|w|=|w'|$ then $wp=w'$ forces
$|p|=0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Geometry: link lengths, spacelikeness, dimension}\label{sec:geom}

\begin{definition}
$\mu(t,u):=Q(u-t)=(u_1-t_1)^2+(u_2-t_2)^2-(u_3-t_3)^2$; positive means spacelike.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Edge spectrum]\label{thm:edges}
For an event $t=(a,b,c)$,
\[
\mu(t,At)=4(c-b)^2,\qquad \mu(t,Bt)=4(a-b)^2,\qquad \mu(t,Ct)=4(c-a)^2 .
\]
The outer identities hold for all integers; the middle one uses $a^2+b^2=c^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:legsne}
No primitive event has $a=b$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$a=b$ with $\gcd(a,b)=1$ forces $a=b=1$ and $c^2=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
At every primitive event all three link lengths are strictly positive: every link is
spacelike.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Polarisation on the cone]\label{lem:polar}
For events $t,u$: $\mu(t,u)=2(t_3u_3-t_1u_1-t_2u_2)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Rigidity of null rays]\label{lem:ray}
Two primitive events with proportional legs are equal.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Total spacelikeness]\label{thm:spacelike}
If $t\ne u$ are primitive events then $\mu(t,u)>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $D:=t_1u_2-u_1t_2$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:ray}, $D\ne 0$. Using $Q(t)=Q(u)=0$ one obtains the
Lagrange-type identity $(t_3u_3)^2=(t_1u_1+t_2u_2)^2+D^2$, so $t_3u_3>t_1u_1+t_2u_2$, and
Lemma~\ref{lem:polar} finishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The order is not ambient causality]
Every tree edge, and indeed every strictly causal pair of the Berggren order, is spacelike
separated in $\RR^{2,1}$: the event set is a total antichain of the ambient causal order, and
$\preceq$ is genealogical rather than Lorentzian.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Pell spine]
$\sigma_k:=B^k\cdot r$, i.e.\ $(3,4,5),(21,20,29),(119,120,169),(697,696,985),\dots$
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Linear interval growth]\label{thm:linear}
$|[r,\sigma_k]|=k+1$ for all $k$, and each such interval is a chain (ordering fraction $1$).
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[No dimension two or more]\label{thm:dim}
There is no $\rho>0$ with $\rho k^2\le |[r,\sigma_k]|$ for all $k\in\NN$; hence no
Myrheim--Meyer dimension $\ge 2$ can be assigned. The one-dimensional bounds
$k\le|[r,\sigma_k]|\le k+1$ hold exactly.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:linear} the requirement reads $\rho k^2\le k+1$, which fails for
$k>2/\rho$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Superpolynomial branching]\label{thm:superpoly}
For every $d\in\NN$ there is $k$ with $k^d<|L_k(r)|=3^k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Pell recurrence]\label{thm:pell}
Along the spine, $s_k:=(\sigma_k)_3$ satisfies $s_{k+2}=6s_{k+1}-s_k$ with $s_0=5$, $s_1=29$,
giving $5,29,169,985,5741,33461,\dots$, growth rate $3+2\sqrt2=(1+\sqrt2)^2$, and
$s_k\ge 5^{k+1}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Under the middle move, $(a+b,c)\mapsto(3(a+b)+4c,\;2(a+b)+3c)$, a linear system with
characteristic polynomial $\lambda^2-6\lambda+1$; eliminating $a+b$ gives the recurrence. The
bound follows by induction from $s_{k+2}=6s_{k+1}-s_k\ge 5s_{k+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cosmic versus proper time]\label{thm:cosmic}
Along one and the same chain, $\tau(r,\sigma_k)=k$, $(\sigma_k)_3\ge 5^{k+1}$ and
$|[r,\sigma_k]|=k+1$.
\end{theorem}

Exponential growth is thus a property of the embedding coordinates, dimension a property of
interval volumes; here the two are decoupled as sharply as possible.

\section{The conformal boundary}\label{sec:boundary}

\begin{definition}[Celestial map]
$\delta(a,b,c):=(a/c,\,b/c)\in\QQ^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
$\delta(t)$ lies on the unit circle, and $\delta$ is injective on primitive events.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The spine is a uniform geodesic]
Along the spine $((\sigma_k)_1-(\sigma_k)_2)^2=1$ for all $k$, because the middle move sends
$a-b\mapsto b-a$; consequently $\mu(\sigma_k,\sigma_{k+1})=4$ for every $k$: the Pell spine is
an infinite chain of equal spacelike steps.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Boundary point]
$(\sigma_k)_1/(\sigma_k)_3 \to \sqrt2/2$, with
$\bigl|\bigl((\sigma_k)_1/(\sigma_k)_3\bigr)^2-\tfrac12\bigr|\le 1/(k+5)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $a,b,c$ the coordinates of $\sigma_k$ and $(a-b)^2=1$, the null condition gives
$2a^2\mp 2a+1=c^2$, hence $|a^2/c^2-\tfrac12|=|2a-1|/(2c^2)\le 1/c\le 1/(k+5)$, using
$c\ge k+5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The endpoint is not an event]
$\sqrt2/2$ is irrational while all celestial coordinates of events are rational; hence no
event sits at the spine's limiting direction, and the conformal boundary strictly extends the
causal set, as null infinity strictly extends Minkowski space.
\end{theorem}

\section{Discussion: why a null cone carries no induced causal order}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:antichain}
Let $X$ be a set of points on the future null cone of $\RR^{d,1}$, no two of which lie on the
same null ray. Then any two distinct points of $X$ are spacelike separated; $X$ is a total
antichain of the ambient causal order.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For null $t,u$, $Q(u-t)=-2\langle t,u\rangle_{\mathrm L}$, and for future-directed null
vectors $\langle t,u\rangle_{\mathrm L}\le 0$ with equality iff they are parallel.
\end{proof}

Primitivity of Pythagorean triples is exactly the hypothesis ``no two on the same ray''.
Consequently any order on the Berggren tree is additional structure, logically independent of
ambient causality; Lorentz equivariance does not help, since the group action moves the
antichain around inside itself. To obtain a Lorentzian causal set from Pythagorean data one
must place events \emph{off} the null cone --- for instance using triples as differences or
momenta rather than positions --- or grade events by an independent time functional whose
level sets are spacelike hypersurfaces of positive-dimensional volume growth.

Three distinct growth rates coexist in the same object:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
quantity & growth\\
\midrule
events at depth $k$ (branching) & exactly $3^k$\\
ambient time coordinate along the Pell spine & $\asymp(3+2\sqrt2)^k$\\
causal interval cardinality at proper time $k$ (volume) & exactly $k+1$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Only the third bears on dimension. The hypothesis conflated the first two with the third.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{(1) Tree enumeration.} Breadth-first expansion of the root by the three matrices;
level $k$ costs $\Theta(3^k)$ operations on integers of $O(k)$ digits, and is duplicate-free
by Theorem~\ref{thm:tree}, so no membership structure is needed.

\textbf{(2) Address decoding.} Apply $P$ to a primitive event; the sign pattern of the result
identifies the move used, then recurse on the true parent. Terminates in $\tau$ steps since
the hypotenuse strictly decreases; cost $O(\tau)$ matrix--vector products. This is the
constructive content of Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqueparent}.

\textbf{(3) Interval computation.} Decode the address of $u$ relative to $t$ and emit the
$\tau+1$ prefixes; optimal by Theorem~\ref{thm:interval}. The Myrheim--Meyer diagnostic then
computes the ordering fraction of $[t,\sigma_k]$, which is identically $1$, pinning the
effective dimension at $1$.

\section{Applications and outlook}

The Berggren causal set is a fully deterministic, exactly enumerable, exactly Lorentz-symmetric
discrete Lorentzian model with exactly computable interval volumes; it is therefore a useful
stress test for dimension estimators, since any estimator reporting $2{+}1$ here is misled by
symmetry or coordinate growth. It also furnishes a canonical separation between exponential
branching, exponential coordinate growth and linear volume growth. The injective celestial map
turns the tree into a structured subset of the rational points of the circle, with the Pell
branch converging to $\sqrt2/2$ at rate $O(1/k)$; other branches converge to other quadratic
irrationalities, suggesting a boundary theory indexed by eventually periodic addresses.
Finally, the freeness result provides an explicit free rank-three submonoid of $O(2,1;\ZZ)$
with a Diophantine certificate of freeness.

Future work: classify boundary points reachable by eventually periodic addresses; construct a
genuinely $2{+}1$-dimensional causal set from Pythagorean data by leaving the null cone;
investigate the stability of the linear-volume verdict under thickenings of the order; and
compute dimension estimates for orders generated by proper subsets of $\{A,B,C\}$.

\section{Conclusion}

Reading $a^2+b^2=c^2$ as $Q(a,b,c)=0$ places every Pythagorean triple on the null cone of
$2{+}1$-dimensional Minkowski space, and the Berggren tree turns this set of points into an
infinite ternary tree carried by an exact integral Lorentz symmetry. The tree, with its
ancestry order, satisfies all order-theoretic axioms of a causal set, with unique addresses,
$3^k$-element antichain levels, chain intervals of size $\tau+1$, exactly computable spacelike
link lengths, and a uniformly spaced Pell geodesic converging to the irrational celestial
direction $\sqrt2/2$. But it is not a discrete spacetime: distinct events are always spacelike
separated and interval volumes are exactly linear, so the effective dimension is $1$, not $3$.
The obstruction is general --- pairwise non-proportional points of a null cone form a total
antichain --- and the outcome is a precise negative theorem in place of a plausible analogy,
together with a fully worked positive structure theory around it.

\end{document}

